    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me summarize the change.\n\nI moved date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, labelled by its ending calendar year), with all values derived from the already business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`.\n\n**New central helper** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/FiscalYear.java`:\n- `of(date)` \u2192 fiscal year as a full calendar year (`month >= 7 ? year+1 : year`)\n- `label(date)` \u2192 `FY<YY>` string\n- `elapsed(from, to)` \u2192 whole fiscal years elapsed\n\n**Changes:**\n- `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` \u2014 the membership number's year segment now uses `FiscalYear.of(...)` instead of the raw registration-date year (e.g. an Aug 2026 registration now yields `...-M27`).\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 added a `fiscalYear` mapping returning `FY<YY>`; replaced the 365-day `tenureDays` check with `tenureFiscalYears` (elapsed fiscal years), awarding the membership point once tenure spans at least one full fiscal year. Removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property (so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the getter/setter) and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\n**Test:** `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsFiscalYear` passes \u2014 `fiscalYear` returns `FY27` for a `2026-08-21` registration, matching `FY\\d{2}`. Full suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 test green.\n\nOne design decision worth flagging: I labelled the fiscal year by the calendar year in which it **ends** (Australian-standard: 1 Jul 2025\u201330 Jun 2026 \u2192 `FY26`), and set the tenure-point threshold at \"\u2265 1 elapsed fiscal year.\" The spec fixed the format and the fiscal-year basis but left these exact conventions open; both are documented in `FiscalYear`, the mapper, and the openapi descriptions. If you'd prefer labelling by the starting year or a different tenure threshold, those are one-line changes.",
